  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $4,$ and the leading coefficient is $6.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}4}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}6}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $\\frac{2}{3}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(2)$ is a factor of $4$ and the denominator $(3)$ is a factor of $6.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
